## Fair Use

You may have "fair use" rights for the software under the law. These terms do not limit them.

## Individuals

Personal hobby, study and independent non-business projects are permitted purposes. All paid own-account work, including freelance work and a one-person automated business, is business use and follows [Small Organizations](#small-organizations), not an unlimited individual exemption. A sole proprietorship counts whether registered or not.

Subject to [Work for Clients](#work-for-clients), work for an organization or whose results belong to it is for that organization's benefit, whoever owns the hardware. An employer is not a client, and employment is not own-account work. Mere use of the public results of your independent personal project does not turn it into organizational work.

## Small Organizations

Internal use for an organization group is a permitted purpose if its R is at most USD 500,000 AND its H is at most 20, using the definitions below. Exactly USD 500,000 and 20 people qualify. All actual users, agents, jobs, machines and instances are unlimited; H counts the whole workforce, not software users. Unattended operation, including CI with no direct human users, is still group use. Public-benefit use and evaluation have the separate permissions below.

Assess R and current H at every month end, and at first adoption using the latest completed month-end assessment (or actual figures since formation if no month end exists). If a genuinely eligible free group later exceeds either limit at a month end, its internal use remains permitted during a 90-calendar-day growth transition starting at that month end. By the deadline it must buy the then-applicable annual license or stop covered use, unless it has regained free eligibility. Re-crossing within 12 months of the original crossing does not restart the allowance: the original deadline still applies. No fees are due for genuinely eligible free use or this authorized transition. A group already above the limits when first adopting does not get growth grace merely by adopting.

Paid groups select the higher of the R and H bands in the fixed schedule incorporated in their commercial order. Ordinary organic growth, including new revenue and newly raised funding, is covered through each paid term year with no mid-year surcharge; renewal uses the latest assessment and permits downgrading or returning to free eligibility. A merger or acquisition requires combined-group reassessment within 90 days of completion and only the prorated positive fee difference for the remaining term, with overlapping prepaid group fees credited. A free group that becomes part of a larger group through a merger or acquisition has the same 90-day period from completion to bring the combined group into compliance. Incorrect initial declarations are corrected to the band due then, not protected as growth. Paid rights expire at the term end without renewal; there is no perpetual internal-use fallback.

## Work for Clients

Any service provider whose own group use is permitted under these terms or a commercial license may use its own tools to produce client deliverables. That remains the provider's use, irrespective of client size, as long as:

1. the software itself runs only on systems you control that the client does not own, rent, pay for (including reimbursing their cost), or control; and

2. neither the client nor anyone acting for it, other than the provider's people doing that work, administers the software or directs an agent through it, for example by writing to it or assigning work to it in an issue tracker. This access test applies regardless of how many days the person works for the client.

Accessing client repositories or systems through separately licensed tools or agents does not itself mean that the software runs there.  A client's request to you is not direction of an agent unless the client can cause agent work through the software without your deciding to pass that request on.

Otherwise, client installation or client-directed operation is the client's use under its own eligibility or commercial license, optionally obtained by the provider on its behalf. Authorized contractors may install, administer and use the client's internal instance without a separate fee for that client-only work; their eligibility to act is not conditional on counting in H. The current-engagement and 30-worked-day rules determine H separately. The provider's separate internal use follows its own group band. There is no royalty on installation, configuration, consulting, maintenance or training invoices; that income still enters the provider's R for its own group assessment.

## Products and Hosted Services

An embedded product includes or runs the software as an operational component, or supplies its agent-orchestration functionality as a feature of another product. Supplying such a product, on premises or hosted, requires a separate written OEM addendum identifying the products, architectures and allowed product-specific tasks, even when the OEM schedule amount is zero. A standard internal-use license alone does not grant these rights. Merely using the software to create output, or an independently written connector or input/output integration that does not supply the software or its functionality, does not require OEM rights.

Offering substantially the software itself as a hosted, managed, rebranded or resale service requires a separate written hosting/reseller agreement and each beneficiary client's own free eligibility or normal group license. It is not covered by a blanket OEM fee. These limits apply regardless of price, host size or recipient size, and cannot be avoided through a new UI, API or AI intermediary.

Internal access by people acting for the group and contractors administering its independently controlled internal instance is not onward hosting. That instance may run on contractor infrastructure. Ordinary client administration or separately supplied generic infrastructure is not itself a provider-controlled software-access service. Classroom, learning and genuine research access under [Education and Public Benefit](#education-and-public-benefit) is expressly permitted without an OEM/hosting agreement; it does not authorize a general-purpose public hosting business. Write to charnesshub@gmail.com; information: https://olosegres.github.io/charness/commercial/.

## Education and Public Benefit

Teaching, student learning and genuine academic or noncommercial scientific research by recognized educational or research institutions and individual researchers are permitted purposes. Internal administration and mission delivery by public or nonprofit educational institutions, genuine charities (including nonprofit public-health, safety and environmental bodies) and public authorities are also permitted, irrespective of core budget, donations, genuine grants, other funding or headcount. Ordinary tuition for recognized education, public appropriations, taxes, statutory fees and cost-recovery public services do not end this exception. Saving costs is not commercial revenue. Industry-funded genuine academic research qualifies; a disguised contract to deliver a proprietary commercial product does not.

A recognized private for-profit school qualifies for actual teaching and learning, not commercial administration or non-educational business. A commercial employer's R&D is ordinary group use, not free academic research merely because a department calls itself a lab.

An otherwise exempt institution's commercial operation using the software remains permitted while its external commercial revenue C is at most USD 500,000 over the measurement period, even if its assigned workforce exceeds 20. If C exceeds that amount, the operation follows the ordinary banded license and growth/renewal rules, using its R and assigned H rather than the institution's exempt core budget and workforce. Funding alone does not trigger this C threshold or remove core-purpose permission. Once C triggers licensing, include investment/commercial-grant funding of the commercial operation in its R, but never core-purpose donations or grants. Connected commercial operations under common institutional control are combined; shared staff doing material work count once, not as fractional FTEs. Unrelated exempt teaching or public-service work remains free.

A separate for-profit spin-off or government-owned trading company follows ordinary group rules from the start, including the H limit. Aggregate related commercial entities under the institutional parent's control, but exclude the exempt core budget and workforce. A nonprofit label does not exempt ordinary commercial trading outside the institutional exception.

## Evaluation

Use to evaluate suitability is permitted during one group-wide period of less than 32 consecutive calendar days, starting when anyone in the group first runs the software for that evaluation. Productive work is allowed only as part of evaluation. Pauses, different people, departments or versions do not restart it. Growth grace does not extend an above-threshold adopter's evaluation. This permission grants no OEM, hosting or resale rights.

## Existing MIT Adopters

MIT grants already received remain unchanged, with no retrospective fees. A group already using an MIT-licensed Charness release before the first release under these terms may use newly licensed releases internally for a single 90-calendar-day transition starting on its first use of a release under these terms. At that deadline it must qualify free, buy the applicable annual license, or stop using the newly licensed releases. It may continue its MIT releases under MIT. This transition and evaluation run concurrently, not consecutively; neither restarts the other or creates a fresh growth allowance for an already above-threshold group. No installation or update by itself removes existing MIT rights. This transition grants no onward product or hosting rights.

## Commercial Licenses

Any use not permitted by these terms needs the corresponding commercial license or separate OEM/hosting/reseller agreement. Authorized no-charge distribution does not authorize otherwise unpermitted operation or modification. Ordinary fees are annual per organization group, with unlimited users, agents and instances, never per seat. To buy, write to charnesshub@gmail.com or use a purchase route listed on the commercial page, https://olosegres.github.io/charness/commercial/.

These terms grant no trademark license and no right to use the name Charness or the licensor's other names or marks, beyond the notices required under [Notices](#notices) and uses the law allows without a license. A commercial order may add a limited contractual permission to display a "Powered by Charness" mark; that permission exists only as stated in the order.

## Eligibility Verification

Organizational and commercial users, including groups claiming free eligibility, must self-assess and retain sufficient eligibility records for three years after the relevant assessment: R calculations, funding totals, aggregate workforce/contractor counts, group structure and applicable commercial-operation or OEM totals. Free ordinary use needs no registration or routine submission. Personal hobbyists' private affairs are outside this clause. Institutions may certify their permitted purpose and relevant commercial-operation bands instead of disclosing their whole budget.

On a credible discrepancy, the licensor may first request an officer's or accountant's written certification, allowing 30 days to respond. Only an unresolved, reasonably grounded discrepancy permits verification by an independent professional bound to confidentiality, at most once per 12 months, on at least 30 days' written notice, during ordinary business hours with minimal interference. Only records relevant to eligibility and fees may be inspected; sufficient redacted accountant confirmations must be accepted. There is no access right to repositories, prompts, model credentials, customer data or production systems. Information may be used only to establish compliance and resolve the discrepancy and must be kept confidential, except where disclosure is legally required.

The licensor pays verification costs unless actual underpayment exceeds BOTH 10% of the fees actually due for the reviewed period AND USD 1,000. Only then may reasonable documented audit costs be recovered, capped at the recovered underpayment. Actual overdue fees for unauthorized use may be collected, but never fees for genuinely free use or authorized transitions, and never a penalty multiplier. First-notice cure under [Violations](#violations) remains intact; a verification request alone is not a violation finding or termination. Prompt honest errors may be corrected, including through a written payment plan agreed with the licensor. Fraud remains subject to ordinary legal remedies. There are no license keys, activation, compliance telemetry or remote shutdown under this licensing model.

